**Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS)** is a common condition that affects how the **ovaries** work. It is thought to affect around **1 in 10 women** and people with ovaries in the UK. Many do not know they have it.

## Symptoms

Symptoms often start in the late teens or early twenties:

- **irregular periods**, or no periods at all
- difficulty getting pregnant, because eggs are not released regularly
- **extra hair** on the face, chest, back or bottom (hirsutism)
- **thinning hair** or hair loss from the head
- **oily skin or acne**
- **weight gain**, or finding it hard to lose weight
- low mood or anxiety

## Why it happens

The exact cause is not known. It often runs in families. It is linked to higher levels of **testosterone** and to **insulin resistance**, where the body does not respond to insulin properly.

## Getting diagnosed

PCOS is usually diagnosed if you have **2 of these 3 things**:

- irregular or no periods
- signs of higher testosterone, from symptoms or a blood test
- polycystic ovaries on an **ultrasound scan** (not used in younger people)

Blood tests also rule out other causes, such as thyroid problems.

## Long-term health

PCOS raises the risk of **type 2 diabetes**, high cholesterol, sleep apnoea and, if you have very few periods, changes to the lining of the womb. Regular checks can pick up problems early.

Read about [PCOS treatment](/conditions/metabolic-and-hormonal/pcos/treatment/).
